Section: Role in Long-lived bat
The negative regulatory region (NRR) of human NOTCH3 maintains the receptor in an autoinhibited state by shielding the S2 cleavage site from ADAM proteases until ligand binding occurs. This region contains three Lin12–Notch repeats (LNRs) and a heterodimerization domain that together prevent premature activation. Comparative sequence analyses show that the long-lived bat Myotis brandtii lacks two short segments within LNR-A and LNR-C. One deleted motif in LNR-A, which normally helps occlude the S2 site, may increase its accessibility to ADAM10. Molecular dynamics simulations indicate that this deletion alone does not cause constitutive signaling but may facilitate a more readily cleavable S2 site and alter how the NRR responds to ligand-induced conformational changes. Although ligand-independent Notch activity is linked to several cancers—most notably involving NOTCH1—the functional consequences of altered NOTCH3 regulation remain comparatively understudied. Bats, including M. brandtii, are exceptional for their longevity relative to body size, yet the contribution of modified intercellular signaling pathways such as Notch to this trait is only beginning to be explored[134].
Section: Notch inhibitors
The involvement of Notch signaling in many cancers has led to investigation of notch inhibitors (especially gamma-secretase inhibitors) as cancer treatments which are in different phases of clinical trials.[2][135] As of 2013[update] at least 7 notch inhibitors were in clinical trials.[136] MK-0752 has given promising results in an early clinical trial for breast cancer.[137] Preclinical studies showed beneficial effects of gamma-secretase inhibitors in endometriosis,[138] a disease characterised by increased expression of notch pathway constituents.[139][140] Several notch inhibitors, including the gamma-secretase inhibitor LY3056480, are being studied for their potential ability to regenerate hair cells in the cochlea, which could lead to treatments for hearing loss and tinnitus.[141][142]
